preConditionHook:
// THE PRIOR SESSION CLOSED STRONG, so the next one opens with a bull bias.
// Brooks's point is about the shape of the DAILY bar: a rally into the close
// makes the day a bull trend bar and biases the following day. Mechanized as
// a flag computed once, at the session boundary, and consulted on every bar
// of the session that follows: the prior session's last close sat within
// toleranceTicks of the highest high of its final closingWindowBars bars. The
// SHORT is the mirror -- a close at the low of that window.
//
// WHEN THE FLAG IS COMPUTED. The last bar of a session cannot be recognised
// while it is printing; it is identifiable exactly one bar LATE, when the
// wall-clock close date changes. That is not a cost here, because the flag is
// not consulted until the next session anyway: on the FIRST bar of the new
// session, close[1] IS the prior session's closing print and bars
// 1..closingWindowBars are its final bars. The merged note offered a
// session-close CLOCK TIME input as the cheaper alternative; the date-change
// form is used instead because it is the standing session model for this
// corpus (EL date > date[1], mirrored as
// day_of(ctx.Time(0)) > day_of(ctx.Time(1)), as SessionOpenPriceTarget writes
// it) and needs no symbol-specific time constant -- an authoring decision;
// the two agree whenever the clock input matches the feed's actual session
// end.
//
// closingWindowMinutes 45 in the book is 9 five-minute bars, hence the
// default 9; the bar-count form survives a change of bar interval where a
// minute count would not. If the prior session was shorter than
// closingWindowBars the window reaches back into the session before it --
// accepted, as the corpus's other bar-count windows do, rather than carrying
// a session-length counter no source asks for.
//
// The window extremes mirror EL's own Extremes(): seeded at the window's
// FIRST (newest) bar, scanned strictly with the tolerance-carrying
// comparison, so a tie keeps the newest bar. Written as a loop rather than
// an EL Highest call offset one bar so the twins read statement for
// statement.
//
// haveBias is the warm-up guard: before the first date change of the run the
// flags still hold their initial false, which is EL's behaviour mirrored, not
// a numerical choice.
//
// NOT CloseRangePosition, which places a close inside its OWN bar's range on
// every bar. This is one reading per session, over a MULTI-BAR window, held
// constant through the next session -- no setting of that rule reproduces it.
// The tick tolerance is the quantity that held the rule for this list; the
// tick is ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale(), division-first, both VERIFIED and
// per-symbol constant. Intraday bars: on a daily or 1440-minute series the
// date changes every bar and the rule degenerates to 'yesterday closed at the
// high of the last closingWindowBars days'.
//
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the
// generator only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration is a
// redefinition when this rule is placed twice in one strategy. The flags and
// the window extremes are the cross-session state and are deliberately NOT
// reset each bar.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
if (day_of(ctx.Time(0)) > day_of(ctx.Time(1))) {
    windowHigh = high[1];
    windowLow = low[1];
    for (int i = 2; i <= (int)closingWindowBars; ++i) {
        if (el_gt(high[i], windowHigh)) windowHigh = high[i];
        if (el_lt(low[i], windowLow)) windowLow = low[i];
    }
    closedStrong = el_ge(close[1], windowHigh - toleranceTicks * tickSize);
    closedWeak = el_le(close[1], windowLow + toleranceTicks * tickSize);
    haveBias = true;
}
